class Solution {
    public int largestAltitude(int[] gain) {
        int ans= 0;  // initially 0
        int max= 0;    // initially 0 
        
        for(int i = 0; i<gain.length; i++) {  // 
           ans +=gain[i]; // ans = ans + gain[i] ---> ans =  0 + (-5) -- > ans = -5
            
            if(ans>max) {    //0 > -5
                max = ans;
            }
        }
        
        return max;
    }
}


// Explanation 

/* First, I create a variable called ans to store the current altitude and initialize it to 0 because the biker starts at altitude 0. Then, I create a variable called max to store the highest altitude found so far, and I also initialize it to 0. Next, I use a for loop to traverse the gain array. In each iteration, I add the current gain to ans to calculate the current altitude. Then, I check whether ans is greater than max. If it is greater, I update max with ans. Finally, I return max, which contains the highest altitude.
*/



// Time Complexity

/*
I use one for loop to traverse the entire array once.
Therefore, the time complexity is O(n).
*/


// Space Complexity

/*
I only use a few integer variables and do not create
any extra array or data structure.

Therefore, the space complexity is O(1).
*/


// Can it be optimized?

/*
No, this solution is already optimal.

We must visit every element at least once to calculate
the altitude, so O(n) is the best possible time complexity.

We also use only constant extra space, so O(1) is already optimal.
